ion solidified even  further a few months ago when this old  veteran set out on a journey to serve  once again seems a friend who works with  a local charity told george there was a  veteran in britain who went on a hundred  walks to support health care workers  george's friend had an idea he said  would you get a hundred walks in by your  birthday can you do that  i said i think so so i did  did he ever for more than three months  leading up to his 100th birthday in the  thin mountain air seven thousand feet  above sea level there he was every day  people come by and say what walk was  this i said oh 31st to the 65th  to my 100th birthday and on his birthday  he's the one who gave the present to his  hometown more than 10 thousand dollars  raised through his walks to help a local  nonprofit serving families during the  pandemic i didn't mind doing it i was  walking anyway and i was able and that's  the important thing of being a veteran  anyway ask anyone around here is to do  what you can to make a difference in  your community they'll tell you he's one  of the very few  world war ii veterans still left from  pearl harbor to his front patio service  is who george blake is thank you again  for your time today it's no wonder  they're all such big fans that would be  a tremendous surprising gift for  veterans voices i'm jeremy hubbard  george had hoped to return to hawaii  this december for the 80th anniversary  ceremony at pearl harbor but a minor  health issue is keeping him home this  year however he still takes those walks  every chance he gets  it's not every day that you get a fire  engine fanfare and a parade for your  birthday but bakersfield resident betty  patrice isn't just anyone she's 106  years old and a world war ii veteran she  jumped into action and enlisted in the  navy after the 1941 attack on pearl  harbor at the time only men could serve  in most naval branches so she joined up  with waves a service that enlisted women  nearly 80 years later she's still  tight-lipped about the classified  projects she dove into she did reveal  the keys to longevity though live a good  clean life and have a happy family  when we think of veterans we often think  about their roles as combat soldiers or  in technical support of war but there  are thousands of other rules some are  musicians born into a musical family  roger dickerson was drafted after  graduating with a master's degree his  new mission to play in the 44th army  band a position he took to heart  you're serving your country and and we  all  were trying to do it the best that we  could and what we did we were musicians  dickerson and the army band spent  significant time in germany playing but  it's when he came home that his work  really took shape he eventually became a  composer and educator training future  generations in the art of music  still ahead one man's mission to honor  heroes  i'm an old army combat engineer ask us  to do something impossible we will try  more on a seemingly impossible task and  why this veteran wants to make sure the  valor of his fellow servicemen is never  forgotten  welcome back search your name on the  internet and you might uncover something  unexpected it happened to army veteran  mark deville and what he found was life  changing he discovered he was a silver  star recipient one of the highest honors  for valor in combat and he never even  knew it that recognition is all thanks  to doug sterner he spends as much as 16  hours a day compiling a massive database  on a website called home of heroes it  documents roughly  275 000 recipients of the nation's  highest military honors  i thought well it would be an impossible  task i said it can't be done but i'm an  old army combat engineer ask us to do  something impossible we will try  sterner says tracking down what he calls  forgotten valor is personal he wanted to  recall the heroism of a friend killed in  vietnam and make sure his bravery was  never lost since then his mission has  been clear give the same due diligence  to those who may have fallen through the  cracks he's rifled through millions of  records over the years doing the  herculean task many thought the military  was already doing while that may be true  it's not an easy job and sometimes not  all veterans want the recognition  when you get a medal for heroism it's  not something you want to talk about  that medal that you got represents the  worst day of your life and that's why  they're lost to history  cerner estimates there are still 150 000  names that need to be added to his  database if you'd like to search his  records for the name of a loved one  visit homeofheroes.com  finally here tonight as we've said  veterans day is quickly approaching on  november 11th there's a woman who wants  to honor the men and women who have  dedicated their lives to our country don  cramer's father who was a korean veteran  is buried at the boulder city memorial  cemetery when kramer visited her dad's  gravesite in 2019 and didn't see flags  she decided to take on the task herself  last year kramer replaced more than 1  000 flags this year she has 5 000 flags  that's so far  and i could come out there on my own  diamond play out there and put three  flights on my dad's grave and call him  today but i can't walk past all those  men and women who fought for my freedom  and gave their service to our country  and and not be able to think great you  know they're being honored today  but you know what it's gonna get done  it's just gonna take time  kramer is looking for help you can call  the local ace hardware store in boulder  city to order more flags  and that's it for us here we thank you  so much for joining us as we share these  incredible voices of veterans each of  them offering a unique snapshot of their  lives and telling stories of bravery  loss and the triumph of the human spirit  we hope you enjoyed and have a wonderful  veterans day i'm sasha loftis good night  [Music]  you
 

